PLACES=[
# ---- Brandon and the west
N('catp-hangar-brandon','Commonwealth Air Training Plan Hangar (Air Training Plan Museum)','Brandon','mb','landmark','standing',49.90364,-99.94280,'exact','open','drive','','1941',
  'A big wooden aircraft hangar at Brandon\'s airport, built in 1941 by Bird Construction of Winnipeg for the No. 12 Service Flying Training School, one of five hangars there. It is one of the few left of about 700 hangars put up to standard plans for the Second World War air training plan. The Manitoba Historical Society says the roof trusses were found to have structural faults in 2022, the hangar was closed, emergency repairs were made and it reopened in June 2023; a full restoration was estimated at $6.3 million.',
  'A very large wood-frame double hangar standing on open prairie beside the airport, now the Commonwealth Air Training Plan Museum, with vintage training aircraft inside. A former canteen building of the flying school also survives on the grounds, and plaques stand on the museum grounds.',
  'Wooden hangar that trained Commonwealth pilots.',
  'The hangar is a municipally designated (2000) and provincially designated (2003, Provincial Heritage Site No. 113) building and houses the Commonwealth Air Training Plan Museum, at 300 Commonwealth Way, east side of Brandon\'s municipal airport on Highway 10, 1 km north of the Trans-Canada Highway. Owner of the building not checked. A parking lot on Commonwealth Way is mapped in OpenStreetMap, beside the hangar (whose lot it is was not checked). Hours from the museum\'s own season notice of March 2026; admission not checked.',
  [MHS('No. 12 Service Flying Training School / CATP Museum','commonwealthairtraininghangar'),PHS('113'),L('Commonwealth Air Training Plan Museum: 2026 season notice','https://airmuseum.ca/2026/03/31/the-catp-museum-opens-for-the-season-on-april-1/'),L('Commonwealth Air Training Plan Museum','https://www.airmuseum.ca/')],
  th=[49.90327,-99.94250],thn='Commonwealth Way lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='Daily 1 p.m. to 4 p.m. from April 1, 2026 (the museum\'s season notice); winter and summer hours not confirmed'),
N('frelsis-lutheran-church-grund','Frelsis (Liberty) Lutheran Church at Grund','Grund (RM of Argyle, near Baldur)','mb','worship','standing',49.46660,-99.24405,'exact','view','drive','','1889',
  'Volunteers under the carpenters Byring Hallgrimsson and Arni Sveinson built this wood-frame church in 1889. The Province calls it the oldest standing Icelandic Lutheran church in Canada. Many of its first members had settled at New Iceland (Gimli) in the late 1870s and then taken up farms in Argyle in the 1880s, still living in log houses when the church went up. In 1964 the Frelsis congregation joined Immanuel Lutheran Church in Baldur (a Historical Society feature gives 1965).',
  'A plain rectangular wooden country church with pointed Gothic windows and delicate tracery on its tower, beside Grund Cemetery. Interior photographs from 2011 exist, so the church survives inside; whether visitors may go in is not checked.',
  'Canada\'s oldest standing Icelandic Lutheran church.',
  'Owned by the Rural Municipality of Argyle (Province\'s record). Parking and how to reach it from the road were not checked; look from the public road. Services and opening times not checked.',
  [PHS('052'),MHS('Frelsis Lutheran Church at Grund','frelsischurchgrund')],
  brick='no',hours=''),
N('clearwater-cpr-water-tower','Clearwater CPR Water Tower','Clearwater (Municipality of Louise)','mb','rail','standing',49.13226,-99.03713,'exact','view','drive','stations','1904 or 1910',
  'An eight-sided wooden water tower of the Canadian Pacific Railway\'s "Standard Number One Plan", which the railway introduced in 1903; 75 were built in Manitoba between 1904 and 1925. Its cedar-lined tank holds 181,840 litres and rests on a frame of big timbers. A coal boiler once ran the pump and kept the water from freezing. Steam engines stopped needing it in 1957, but the tower still supplies water to Clearwater. The sources disagree on its date: the Province\'s page implies the 1904 to 1925 building run, the Historical Society says 1910.',
  'A tall octagonal timber tower beside the railway line with a ball float on a pole above it to show the water level, and a commemorative plaque. It is working, so it cannot be entered.',
  'A steam-age water tower that still works.',
  'Owned by the Rural Municipality of Louise (Province\'s record), on Eleventh Street. It is in use; look from the street and the public way. Parking not checked.',
  [PHS('093'),MHS('CPR Water Tower, Clearwater','clearwatertower')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours=''),
# ---- Parkland and the north
N('negrych-pioneer-homestead','Wasyl Negrych Pioneer Homestead','Venlaw (Municipality of Gilbert Plains)','mb','landmark','standing',51.30710,-100.44973,'site','open','drive','','1897–1990',
  'Wasyl Negrych and Anna Verhun came from the Carpathian foothills of western Ukraine in 1897 with seven children and took up land here, building first poplar-pole shelters. They put up the main house in 1899 in the style of their homeland. Descendants lived on the farm until 1990 with no electricity, running water or telephone. The Manitoba Historical Society calls the house the oldest known Ukrainian-Canadian dwelling still on its original site. The farm was made a Provincial Heritage Site in 1992 and a National Historic Site in 1996 (Parks Canada\'s database gives 1997).',
  'The 1899 house of squared tamarack logs with white mud plaster inside and out, a log bunkhouse of 1908 with a working clay-and-log stove (a peech), a cow barn, granaries and other log buildings in a clearing by the Drifting River, with a garden and orchard. Two more homesteads of Wasyl\'s brothers stand a short walk north.',
  'The best-kept Ukrainian farm in Canada, still in its clearing.',
  'Owned by the Municipality of Gilbert Plains and leased to the Gilbert Plains and District Historical Society. About 17 km north of Gilbert Plains, then east on Negrych Road; the pin is on the clearing and the buildings are almost half a mile from the nearest public road along a winding driveway. Open July and August on the society\'s page (daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.); a town page quoted in a web search says Tuesday to Saturday, which was not opened here, so the days are not certain. Other times by appointment. Admission and parking not checked.',
  [MHS('Wasyl Negrych Pioneer Homestead','negrychhomestead'),PHS('067'),L('Gilbert Plains Historical Society: Negrych homestead','https://negrychpioneerhomestead.blogspot.com')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='July and August, daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. (the society\'s page; a town page says Tuesday to Saturday); other times by appointment'),
N('dauphin-cn-engine-house','Canadian National engine house and turntable','Dauphin','mb','rail','standing',51.15381,-100.06224,'site','view','drive','','1906',
  'A railway engine house and turntable of 1906 built for the line that became the Canadian National, on Jackson Street in Dauphin. The Manitoba Historical Society shows the building standing in photographs and an aerial view of 2021, with the turntable in front of it. Who owns it and whether it is in use were not found.',
  'A fan-shaped engine house with the turntable pit in front of it, seen from Jackson Street. What it is built of was not checked. Interior and condition not checked.',
  'A prairie engine house with its turntable.',
  'Owner and access not found; look from Jackson Street and stay out of the railway property. Parking not checked.',
  [MHS('CNR turntable and engine house, Dauphin','dauphinroundhouse')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours=''),
N('our-lady-seven-sorrows-camperville','Our Lady of Seven Sorrows Church','Camperville (RM of Mountain)','mb','worship','standing',52.00388,-100.15166,'exact','view','drive','','1910–1912',
  'Oblate missionaries worked among Indigenous and Métis people on the west shore of Lake Winnipegosis, and the congregation built this church between 1910 and 1912, probably to a design by Father St. Germain. The Province says no other Roman Catholic mission church in Manitoba was as large. Fire destroyed the interior in 1930 and it was rebuilt. It became a Provincial Heritage Site in 1991.',
  'A large church in the Quebec manner (the Province says such churches have imposing size and carefully designed fronts), with its cemetery beside it; what it is built of was not checked. A monument to the Pine Creek Residential School, which the Oblates ran nearby, stands next to the church. Interior photographs exist; whether visitors may go in is not checked.',
  'The largest Catholic mission church in Manitoba.',
  'Owned by the Roman Catholic Archiepiscopal Corporation of Winnipeg (Province\'s record). Parking and service times not checked; respect the cemetery and the memorial.',
  [PHS('057'),MHS('Our Lady of Seven Sorrows, Camperville','ourladyofsevensorrows')],
  brick='?',hours=''),
N('fort-dauphin-monument-winnipegosis','Fort Dauphin monument','Winnipegosis (RM of Mossey River)','mb','curio','marker',51.65535,-99.92339,'area','view','drive','forts','1741',
  'A stone monument with a federal plaque (unveiled August 17, 1952) marks the place where Fort Dauphin is believed to have been built in the autumn of 1741 by a son of the explorer La Vérendrye, on Lake Winnipegosis. The Hudson\'s Bay Company later reused the site in the winter of 1795 to 1796 (Wikipedia). Nothing of the fort is known to stand; the fort\'s exact site is not settled.',
  'A stone monument with a plaque. No fort remains are described.',
  'A cairn for a fur-trade fort of 1741.',
  'In the town of Winnipegosis, among houses and lots on the aerial; owner of the ground and parking not checked, so look from the street.',
  [MHS('Fort Dauphin Monument','fortdauphin'),W('Fort Dauphin (Manitoba)')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours=''),
# ---- Interlake and the east
N('manitoba-glass-company-beausejour','Manitoba Glass Company site','Beausejour','mb','industry','traces',50.05303,-96.52311,'site','public','drive','','1906–1914',
  'The first glass-container factory in western Canada. Joseph Keilbach and partners began building it in June 1906. Glassblowers from Poland and the United States made bottles for Winnipeg breweries and soft-drink firms, and by 1909 to 1911 new machines made jars and medicine and ink bottles too; at its peak it employed 350 people. It could not compete with the large eastern makers that held the licence for fully automatic machines, and a Montreal firm bought it and moved the work to Redcliff, Alberta. The Beausejour works closed by 1914.',
  'No buildings are left, but the concrete foundations can still be seen, with the Manitoba Heritage Council plaque of 1989.',
  'The foundations of the prairies\' first bottle factory.',
  'Owned by the Town of Beausejour (Province\'s record), on Cemetery Road West beside the cemetery; the federal public-land map does not show it. No parking lot is mapped nearby; the foundations lie among trees on the aerial, so they may be hard to see in summer. Parking not checked.',
  [MHS('Manitoba Glass Company Site','manitobaglasscompany'),PHS('041')],
  brick='no',hours=''),
N('gabels-general-store-ladywood','Gabel\'s General Store','Ladywood (RM of Brokenhead)','mb','landmark','standing',50.18009,-96.49724,'exact','view','drive','','1929–1931',
  'A two-storey wood country store built in 1929 to 1931 for Henry and Mary Gabel to a design by Roy Millbrant of Beausejour, with a false front, stepped gable and dormers. The Gabels ran it until 1975. The Province calls it one of the best surviving examples of a boomtown-style country store, with the floors, oak columns, display cases, scale, vault and drink cooler original. It was once a museum with a 1930s store theme and an artist\'s studio; the Historical Society notes that the verandahs on the south side were taken off between 2019 and 2023.',
  'A tall false-fronted wooden store with a stepped gable. Visible changes since the Province\'s description: the verandahs are gone. Interior not checked and its present use is not confirmed.',
  'A boomtown store with the counters still in it.',
  'Privately owned (Province\'s record). Look from the public road; whether the museum still opens is not checked, so ask first if you want to go in.',
  [PHS('062'),MHS('Gabel\'s General Store, Ladywood','gabelsgeneralstore')],
  brick='no',hours=''),
N('st-elias-sirko','St. Elias Ukrainian Orthodox Church (the log church)','Sirko (RM of Stuartburn)','mb','worship','standing',49.01524,-96.24899,'exact','view','drive','','1908–1910',
  'Settlers began this log church in 1908 under Dmytro Waskul, and Wasyl Cheredaryk built the interior in 1910. It is one of the best examples of the Bukovynian church style in Canada, small enough for only about 30 standing adults. Its screen of icons (the iconostasis) is covered with Russian lithographs from before the revolution. A log bell tower of traditional build and a cemetery with tall white crosses stand beside it. The same congregation built a larger church in 1950 about 125 m away, so the two form Manitoba\'s only surviving pair of Ukrainian churches from two generations.',
  'A tiny log church with rounded ends, painted white and blue inside, a log bell tower and the cemetery; the 1950 three-domed church stands nearby. Whether visitors may go in is not checked.',
  'A 30-person log church beside its successor.',
  'Owned by the trustees of the congregation (Province\'s record), in farmland near Sirko. A Manitoba Heritage Council plaque stands by the church. Parking and service times not checked.',
  [MHS('St. Elias church, bell tower and cemetery (the 1908 church)','steliasukrainianorthodox'),MHS('St. Elias (the 1950 church)','steliasukrainianorthodoxnew'),PHS('121'),PHS('118')],
  brick='no',hours=''),
# ---- Winnipeg
N('fort-garry-hotel-winnipeg','Fort Garry Hotel','Winnipeg (Downtown)','mb','hotel','standing',49.88795,-97.13689,'exact','open','walk','hotels','1911–1913',
  'A railway hotel built by the Grand Trunk Pacific between 1911 and 1913 and opened in December 1913, designed by the Montreal architects George A. Ross and David H. MacFarlane in the Château style. It is the last of Winnipeg\'s grand railway hotels and Manitoba\'s only Château-style example. The Historical Society says a federal plaque was unveiled in 1980 (Wikipedia gives its national designation as 1981) and that the province designated it in 1990. A 2026 conservation award was for the Oval Room, the mezzanine, the Vice-Regal Suite and the limestone and copper roof.',
  'A tall limestone hotel with a copper roof, beside the remains of Upper Fort Garry\'s gate and the heritage park, one block from Union Station. Banquet halls and rooms inside are described as well kept; the hotel is working, so the public rooms are open to guests and event visitors, not on a fixed tour.',
  'The last grand railway hotel in Winnipeg.',
  'A working hotel at 222 Broadway. Street and paid parking nearby (not checked). The hotel\'s own page was not read, so hours are not shown.',
  [MHS('Fort Garry Hotel','fortgarryhotel'),PHS('048'),W('Fort Garry Hotel')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('union-station-winnipeg','Union Station','Winnipeg (Downtown)','mb','rail','standing',49.88885,-97.13456,'exact','open','walk','stations','1908–1912',
  'A four-storey Beaux-Arts station of Tyndall limestone on Main Street, designed by the New York architects Warren and Wetmore, who also designed Grand Central Terminal. It was built between 1908 and 1911 by Lyall-Mitchell for the Grand Trunk Pacific and Canadian Northern railways, at a cost of about $1,000,000. The first train came in on August 7, 1911 and it opened officially on June 24, 1912, once the train sheds were done. It welcomed thousands of immigrants to the prairies. A federal plaque inside the rotunda was unveiled in 1976, and the rotunda was renovated in 2014.',
  'A long limestone front on Main Street with an iron canopy over the doors and a dome on top, and the domed rotunda inside, still a working VIA Rail station. A railway museum once occupied part of the building; whether it has reopened is not checked.',
  'A palace of a station for the Prairies\' immigrants.',
  'VIA Rail station at 123 Main Street, beside The Forks. Station doors per VIA Rail\'s page. Parking not checked.',
  [MHS('Union Railway Station','unionstation'),W('Union Station (Winnipeg)'),L('VIA Rail: Winnipeg station','https://www.viarail.ca/en/stations/prairies-and-northern-manitoba/winnipeg')],
  brick='no',hours='Station open Monday and Thursday 6 a.m. to 10 p.m.; Wednesday and Sunday 6 a.m. to midnight; Tuesday, Friday and Saturday 6 a.m. to 5 p.m. (VIA Rail)'),
# ---- the far north
N('archway-warehouse-norway-house','Archway Warehouse, Jail and Powder Magazine','Norway House','mb','fort','standing',53.98177,-97.83584,'exact','view','drive','','1837–1856',
  'Norway House was the crossroads of the Hudson\'s Bay Company\'s northern transport routes for much of the 1800s. Three buildings of the post survive: the Archway Warehouse of 1840 to 1841, the oldest Red River frame warehouse in western Canada and the oldest log building in Manitoba still on its original ground; the jail of 1855 to 1856, the oldest surviving lockup in the province; and the powder magazine of 1837 to 1838, the first stone building in Manitoba, now in ruins. The warehouse bell came from the company ship Sea Horse III (1782 to 1792). Treaty No. 5 was made here in 1875.',
  'The log warehouse with its bell and weather-vane, the small jail, and the stone ruin of the powder magazine, in the community of Norway House. A 1931 federal plaque commemorates the post.',
  'Three surviving buildings of the fur trade\'s northern crossroads.',
  'Owned by Northern Stores Inc. (Province\'s record, Provincial Heritage Site No. 12, designated 1984). Road access, parking and whether the buildings can be entered were not checked; look from outside and ask in the community first.',
  [MHS('Archway Warehouse, Jail and Powder Magazine Remains','warehouseremains'),PHS('012')],
  brick='no',hours=''),
]

